{"title":"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 14","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A <i>palindrome</i> is a positive integer that is the same when read forwards or backwards. For example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/d/52d4135e81554e012372b44b3ccb53f4a918c9f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$545$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/6/a46e70d0f053b6dcdd6f561fd6f65abb888175fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1331$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> are both palindromes. The difference between the smallest three-digit palindrome and the largest three-digit palindrome is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/0/4d0c9882bd94e316e8782268fb0f5773555201ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{ (A) }\\ 909 \\qquad\\text{ (B) }\\ 898 \\qquad\\text{ (C) }\\ 888 \\qquad\\text{ (D) }\\ 979 \\qquad\\text{ (E) }\\ 878$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"479\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>From the description of a palindrome, the largest three-digit palindrome is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/0/0705411d92671faf1fe5602cfef8f353c7f0f83d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$999$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> and the smallest three-digit palindrome is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/b/94bb6fb7a3ff722532798b45607025254679e7b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$101$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />. The difference between the two is then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/d/a9d0546ac95559ea722aca55fce79fa07d3715c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$999 - 101 = \\boxed{\\textbf {(B) } 898}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"176\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)\">2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 13\">Problem 13</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 15\">Problem 15</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/ind